= Digital organism — Simple =

A '''digital organism''' is a self-replicating computer program used to study evolution.

A digital organism usually has:

* a digital genome;
* a way to reproduce;
* mutations;
* competition for resources;
* descendants that inherit information.

This allows a population of programs to evolve.

== Digital genome ==

A digital genome can be a sequence of instructions.

The organism executes those instructions inside a virtual environment.

The instructions can control:

* copying;
* movement through memory;
* calculations;
* interactions.

When the organism reproduces, mutations can change the instruction sequence.

== Selection ==

If some digital organisms reproduce more successfully than others, their genomes become more common.

Selection can favor organisms that:

* copy faster;
* use resources better;
* perform rewarded tasks;
* exploit another organism.

Fitness depends on the digital environment.

== Tierra ==

Thomas Ray's Tierra was an early influential digital-evolution system.

Self-replicating programs competed for memory and processor time.

Evolution produced smaller replicators and parasite-like strategies.

Researchers did not explicitly program every evolved strategy.

== Avida ==

Avida is another major platform.

Avida organisms execute instructions, mutate, reproduce, and evolve.

Researchers can record complete lineages and every mutation.<ref name="avida">Ofria, C. and Wilke, C. O. (2004), “Avida: A Software Platform for Research in Computational Evolutionary Biology.”</ref>

This makes Avida useful for controlled evolutionary experiments.

== Digital evolution is real inside the system ==

If descendants inherit variation and reproductive success differs, Darwinian selection can occur.

The evolution is real relative to the digital environment.

The environment is still artificial.

Its instruction set, resource rules, and mutation system were designed by researchers.

== Digital organisms are not biological organisms ==

Digital organisms do not have biological:

* cells;
* metabolism;
* DNA;
* chemistry.

They share some abstract properties of biological evolution.

Calling them “organisms” is therefore a modeling convention.

== Digital organism versus computer virus ==

A computer virus can copy itself.

But viruses are normally discussed as cybersecurity threats.

Digital organisms are usually created inside controlled research systems to study evolution.

The technical properties can overlap, but the purpose is different.

== Digital organism versus AI agent ==

An AI agent can solve problems without reproducing.

A digital organism can reproduce and evolve without being intelligent.

The concepts are different.

== Ecology ==

Digital organisms can interact.

They may:

* compete;
* parasitize;
* cooperate;
* depend on one another.

This creates artificial ecological dynamics.

== Open-ended evolution ==

Researchers would like artificial populations to keep generating new kinds of adaptations.

This is called '''open-ended evolution'''.

It is difficult.

Most digital systems eventually stabilize, repeat, or run out of new opportunities.

== Experimental advantage ==

Researchers can create many identical starting populations.

Then they can change one factor, such as mutation rate.

They can compare the outcomes.

This makes digital organisms useful for experimental evolution.

== Limits ==

Results from digital evolution do not automatically generalize to biological life.

The digital world can omit:

* chemistry;
* development;
* physical constraints;
* ecological complexity.

Digital organisms are models.

They are useful precisely because the rules are explicit.

== Consciousness ==

A program can evolve without being conscious.

Replication, mutation, and selection do not prove subjective experience.

